### Confirmed accounting gap, not an observed test-run failure

The published step declares exactly one created UI-test identity. The current
source adds that fact plus three separate picker facts. Independent read-only
replay of the accepted inventory, ledger and operator effects through step 118
confirms that all three extra identities are absent from its expected census:

- `the library folder picker resolves its start folder and delivers only the first selected folder on the UI thread`
- `cancelling the library folder picker leaves the selection unchanged`
- `an unavailable storage provider leaves the library folder selection unchanged`

Their class is `OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests.AppContextTests`. The existing
operator-effects file contains only the step-52 rename. `check_observed_tests`
compares identities, not just class membership or counts. Once the fixture
compiles, discovery of these facts would therefore introduce a further blocker
unless their authority is recorded. No 0118 UI test run has yet observed that
failure; this is a confirmed discrepancy in the declared expected set.

This misunderstanding recurs from 0046 and 0108. At 0108, extra behavioral
checks were eventually composed into an authorized acceptance test while their
assertions were preserved. That repair does not authorize extra identities at
0118. Here the operator can retain the clearer separate facts and record three
step-118 `create` effects, without amending the published ledger or weakening
identity checks. The actual behavioral coverage should be preserved either way.

### Additional ASG defect: documentation classified as provider authentication failure

The log records `QUOTA_HIT kind=auth_failure` for implementation attempt 02
at 09:43:10 UTC and for the successful postmortem at 09:58:38 UTC. Both runs
exited 0. Their reported matching text is Avalonia XML documentation containing
`T:System.UnauthorizedAccessException`.

A read-only reproduction passed exactly
`<exception cref="T:System.UnauthorizedAccessException" />` to each of the
OpenAI, Anthropic and Kimi providers' `parse_events` functions. All three returned
`auth_failure`. Each authentication regex contains an unqualified `unauthorized`
alternative and scans transcript content. This is a shared tool defect, not an
authentication problem in the account. It did not terminate this run, but it
pollutes diagnostics and can mislead other callers or subsequent analyses.

The primary report's statement that ASG source changes are unnecessary is true
for removing the immediate compiler error; it does not cover this independently
verified defect. A provider change, token purchase or reauthentication is not a
remedy supported by this incident.

